NAME
    vsed - edit sensitive text in memory using a terminal UI

SYNOPSIS
    vsed
    vsed <file>

DESCRIPTION
    vsed is a minimal visual stream editor for sensitive text.

    With no operand, vsed reads standard input when it is not a terminal. If
    standard input is a terminal, vsed starts immediately with an empty
    document. Saving writes the result to standard output.

    With one file operand, vsed loads an existing readable/writable regular
    file. If the pathname does not exist, vsed starts with an empty document
    and creates that file only when the session is saved.

    The interactive interface uses /dev/tty. UI text is never written to stdout.

INITIAL TEXT DOMAIN
    The first implementation accepts NUL-free text containing only:

        printable 7-bit ASCII
        TAB
        LF line separators

    Other input is outside the first-delivery contract. NUL-free input is a
    caller precondition: portable POSIX shell variables cannot represent NUL,
    so this implementation does not claim deterministic NUL rejection.
    Detectable non-ASCII/control input is rejected. TAB is preserved but is
    rendered with a one-column '>' placeholder.

KEYS
    Printable ASCII
        Insert at the cursor.

    Tab
        Insert a TAB.

    Enter
        Split the current line.

    Backspace
        Delete the previous character, joining lines at line start.

    Delete
        Delete the current character, joining the next line at line end.

    Left / Right
        Move one character, crossing line boundaries.

    Up / Down
        Move between logical lines.

    Home / End
        Move to the start/end of the current line.

    PageUp / PageDown
        Move approximately one visible page.

    Ctrl-X
        Save and exit successfully.

    Escape
        Cancel and exit without saving.

MEMORY-ONLY SECURITY
    vsed does not create plaintext temporary, swap, backup, undo, journal or
    recovery files and does not invoke an external editor.

    The editable document is held in non-exported process-local shell state.
    Shell xtrace and verbose tracing are disabled before document content is
    loaded.

    In stream mode, saved plaintext is written only to stdout. In file mode,
    saved plaintext is written only to the selected file. A new selected file
    is created under umask 077 only when the session is saved; cancellation
    leaves the pathname absent.

    File-mode save is deliberately direct rather than atomic: avoiding a
    plaintext staging file means an I/O failure during save can leave a
    partially written target.

    vsed clears/leaves its alternate screen and unsets document variables on
    normal/trapped cleanup where possible. Portable POSIX shell cannot provide
    guaranteed memory zeroization.

SECURITY BOUNDARY
    The command cannot guarantee against operating-system paging/swap,
    hibernation, external core-dump policy, process-memory inspection, terminal
    emulator capture/history, kernel/device buffers, host-shell implementation
    internals, or physical memory remanence.

    Input control bytes are not emitted as terminal control sequences because
    they are outside the supported first-delivery text domain.

SAVE FIDELITY
    Within the supported text domain, an unchanged save preserves empty input,
    empty lines, TAB bytes, and the presence or absence of the final LF.

OPERANDS
    file
        Existing readable/writable regular file to edit in place, or a
        non-existing pathname to create on save from an initially empty
        document.

EXIT STATUS
    0
        Saved successfully.

    1
        Editing cancelled by the user.

    2
        Invalid invocation, unsupported input, terminal/runtime failure, or
        save failure.

    129 / 130 / 131 / 141 / 143 / 148
        Handled HUP, INT, QUIT, PIPE, TERM and TSTP respectively. Terminal
        cleanup is attempted before the signal-derived status is returned.

FILES
    /dev/tty
        Interactive terminal input and visual output.

    vsed does not create a temporary, swap, backup, undo, journal or recovery
    file.

